# Framework Troubleshooting Mode

Use for explicit troubleshooting and repeated failures of project-managed
resources. AGENTS.md supplies the ambient trigger; this file owns the project
adapter. The portable procedure is the global skill
[interactive-troubleshooting-to-managed-state](../../../global-skills/skills/implementation/interactive-troubleshooting-to-managed-state/SKILL.md),
discoverable through the personal runtime skill roots. Do not copy its full
workflow into another project skill.

## Project route

Enter `homelab-ansible-first-entry` once and select its repair door. Read desired
state and identify the failing application, host, user, shell mode and invocation
before choosing the fix. Use `bin/codex-env` for project Ansible/Python execution,
inventory-defined targets and the owning role/playbook. Existing host, secret,
execution and user-authorization gates remain in effect.

Bounded reversible experiments are allowed within repair authority **before the
failing Ansible task is known**. Capture baseline, mutation ownership and undo;
update the owning project; undo only experiment-owned changes and verify the
baseline; apply through Ansible; verify original-context behavior; apply again
and repeat behavior verification. Never undo concurrent/unrelated changes.
If returning production to failure is unsafe, use an isolated reproduction and
state its limits. No persistent experiment means no artificial rollback.

## Evidence proportionality

The default artifact is one compact receipt with exact command/context, exit
code, relevant output or saved raw-output links, and temporary mutation ledger.
The global skill supplies `assets/receipt.md`. A useful existing collector should
be used. A new collector/playbook pair is warranted by recurrence, complexity,
evidence volume, or a gap that compact probes cannot resolve; it is not a
prerequisite for ordinary diagnosis or a small fix.

### Host thermal / cooling (do not skip)

Before interpreting fan noise or “high workload” acoustics on Hyper-V GPU
hosts, load inventory `cooling_contract` (see
`docs/reference/host-thermal-cooling-contracts.md`):

| Host | Cooling | Trap |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `HOM-LAB-HVH-02` | **Liquid** (Corsair radiator curves) | `nvidia-smi fan.speed == 0` does **not** prove thermal idle |
| `HOM-LAB-HVH-01` | **Air** | GPU/case fan RPM are valid proxies with temp/power |

Prefer GPU temperature, power draw, utilization, VRAM residency, Hyper-V guest
CPU, and inference logs over GPU onboard fan percent on liquid-cooled hosts.
Radiator fan ramp after a finished workload is expected cooldown.

Inspect component-native output first where available, then explicit command
results and Ansible module output. Save the evidence before assessing it.
Task names and play recaps alone establish neither behavior nor convergence.
Read errors fully, explain their meaning, and change the hypothesis or probe
before retrying. Follow AGENTS.md's three-informed-attempt ceiling. Repeated
failure calls for narrower evidence or targeted research, not a blind retry or
an automatic collector-building detour.

## Verification and closeout

Keep these claims separate: reproduced, experimental baseline restored, project
fix applied, original behavior verified, second apply repeatable. A different
shell is substitute evidence; an untested original app keeps behavior pending.
A zero-change run with failing behavior is a failed repair. Never hide drift
with changed_when. Explain genuinely volatile tasks separately.

Use concise updates with the key raw output or evidence link, assessment and
next probe. Final receipt includes commands, results, source changes, rollback
status and material limits. Do not require a fixed multi-heading diagnostic
report for every command. Ordinary repairs need no new plan or library packet;
existing user-approved plan obligations still apply.

## Existing collectors

Use their existing task/playbook interfaces when relevant:

| Component | Dedicated playbook | Tag |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Hyper-V Ubuntu VM | `playbooks/troubleshoot/collect_hyperv_ubuntu_vm_artifacts.yaml` | `collect_hyperv` |
| Hyper-V Ubuntu GPU-P | `playbooks/troubleshoot/collect_hyperv_ubuntu_gpu_p_artifacts.yaml` | `collect_hyperv_gpu_p` |
| Windows remote access | `playbooks/troubleshoot/collect_windows_remote_access_artifacts.yaml` | `collect_windows_remote_access` |
| Cursor shell PATH | `playbooks/troubleshoot/collect_cursor_shell_path_artifacts.yaml` | `collect_cursor_shell_path` |
| Host thermal / cooling | `playbooks/troubleshoot/collect_host_thermal_cooling_artifacts.yaml` | `collect_host_thermal` |

Artifacts live under `artifacts/troubleshooting/`. For a warranted new collector,
use `roles/troubleshooting_collectors/tasks/<component>.yml`, a thin dedicated
playbook, and an opt-in tag in the owning playbook. Capture only relevant,
secret-safe output. `-vvv` is available when Ansible execution details are useful;
mention other diagnostic flags/tags only when the component implements them.

## Explicit one-off requests

Manual execution does not waive project stewardship. Record every explicit
one-off request using `docs/one_off_tasks/README.md`; that file owns the record
format, debt/deferral disposition and retention rules. Honor manual-only scope.
